.TH PYCDLIB-DIFF 1 "Nov 2026" "pycdlib-diff"

.SH NAME
pycdlib-diff - tool to compare two ISO images using pycdlib

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pycdlib-diff [OPTIONS] <old-iso-file> <new-iso-file>

.SH DESCRIPTION
This is a tool to compare two ISO images and report a classified diff.  The
two images may store the same file data at completely different locations, so
only the contents and the relationships between entries are compared, never
the raw data locations.  The identity of entries is decided by explicit
directory evidence (the path within the directory hierarchy) together with
content digests: entries at the same path are considered the same object even
when their contents changed, while entries with identical contents at
different paths are considered migrations.  The reported sections are:
content updates (same path, different contents), path migrations (same
contents, different path), directory attribute changes (same directory, only
metadata such as the recording date or Rock Ridge permissions changed),
shared block changes (the extent-sharing, i.e. hard-link, relationship
between entries changed), additions, and deletions.  Deletions can only be
inferred from a complete old image; if the old image is truncated or declared
partial, deletions are not reported.  The exit code is 0 when there are no
differences, 1 when there are differences, and 2 on error.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-path\-type " [auto,iso,rockridge,joliet,udf]"
Specifies the path name convention to use while comparing the images.  If
either ISO doesn't contain the path type specified, an error is thrown.  If
not specified, defaults to auto, which first tries the UDF path, then the
Rock Ridge path, then the Joliet path, and finally falls back to the ISO9660
path if all else fails.
.TP
.B \-\-old\-is\-partial
Declares the old image incomplete.  When the old image is not complete,
deletions cannot be inferred (entries missing from the new image may simply
be unreadable in the old one), so they are not reported.  Truncated old
images are detected automatically; this flag is useful when the old image is
known to be partial for other reasons.

.SH SEE ALSO
pycdlib-explorer(1), pycdlib-extract-files(1), pycdlib-genisoimage(1)

